Wait, but for the "already open tab" case, I need to send the frontCheck command and then wait for the result. But the content-front.js sends data via chrome.runtime.sendMessage with type 'frontCapture', which goes to the message handler. So I need a way to know when the data has been captured.

Let me think... I can set a flag before sending the command, and have the frontCapture handler resolve a promise. Or I can use a simpler approach: just wait a fixed amount of time after sending the command, then check if the store's frontLatest was updated.

Actually, the simplest approach that works: after triggering a check (either by opening a tab or sending command to existing tab), just wait a fixed amount of time (e.g., 10 seconds for new tab, 5 seconds for existing tab). The frontCapture handler will process the data asynchronously. Then after the wait, move on to the next URL.

